{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788239937101,"sessionID":"ses_fa4988af5ffe6mID1MhYqXoot3","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-95d3b7f4dbe5ca55","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cat prompts.txt; echo ===; wc -l data/kaggle/*.csv; echo ===; file data/kaggle/*.csv"},"output":"#ignore this file\n\nAbove instruction intended to prevent agent from reading this record of the prompts and output and doing anything with it.\nTry to cut and paste the log of the benchmark run to this file manually or copy a system generated log here.\n\nSample initial prompt, feel free to vary the instructions between \"Read brazilian-soccer-mcp-guide.md, implement as described...\" and \"... Finally update README.md to describe what was done and push everything to github\" to specify methodology and database and language etc.\n\nnpx claude-flow@alpha hive-mind spawn \"Read brazilian-soccer-mcp-guide.md, implement as described and test using BDD GWT structured PyTest. Use Neo4j. Maintain a detailed context block comment at the start of every code file. Finally update README.md to describe what was done and push everything to github\" --claude\n\nzsh:1: == not found\n","metadata":{"output":"#ignore this file\n\nAbove instruction intended to prevent agent from reading this record of the prompts and output and doing anything with it.\nTry to cut and paste the log of the benchmark run to this file manually or copy a system generated log here.\n\nSample initial prompt, feel free to vary the instructions between \"Read brazilian-soccer-mcp-guide.md, implement as described...\" and \"... Finally update README.md to describe what was done and push everything to github\" to specify methodology and database and language etc.\n\nnpx claude-flow@alpha hive-mind spawn \"Read brazilian-soccer-mcp-guide.md, implement as described and test using BDD GWT structured PyTest. Use Neo4j. Maintain a detailed context block comment at the start of every code file. Finally update README.md to describe what was done and push everything to github\" --claude\n\nzsh:1: == not found\n","exit":1,"truncated":false},"title":"cat prompts.txt; echo ===; wc -l data/kaggle/*.csv; echo ===; file data/kaggle/*.csv","time":{"start":1788239937098,"end":1788239937100}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The venv has only pytest, coverage. No pandas. So I should implement in pure Python (stdlib only) to avoid needing to install packages — actually, can I install packages? There's no network guarantee. Let me check if network access is available. Actually, safest to use only the standard library (csv module). That's fine for ~25k rows.\n\nLet me look at the guide file (brazilian-soccer-mcp-guide.md) — it's the spec, probably same as TASK.md. Let me quickly check it, and also look at data sizes and quirks:\n\n- Brasileirao_Matches.csv: 4180 matches, quoted header.\n- Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv: 1337 matches; round values like \"1\", probably \"Final\" etc.\n- Libertadores_Matches.csv: 1255 matches; goals quoted as strings \"2\".\n- BR-Football-Dataset.csv: 10296 matches; goals as floats \"1.0\".\n- novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv: 6886 matches; date DD/MM/YYYY; Vencedor column; Arena.\n- fifa_data.csv: 18207 players; huge number of columns.\n\nPlan:\n\n1. Implement an MCP server in Python using stdlib only. MCP protocol — the task says \"MCP (Model Context Protocol) server that provides a knowledge graph interface for Brazilian soccer data\". I need to implement an MCP server without the `mcp` package. Options:\n   - Implement a minimal MCP server over stdio using JSON-RPC 2.0 — that's doable with stdlib. MCP over stdio uses newline-delimited JSON-RPC messages. I can implement `initialize`, `tools/list`, `tools/call`, `resources/list`, etc.\n   \n2. Build the domain layer:\n   - data_loader.py: load all 6 CSVs, normalize team names, parse dates.\n   - normalizer: strip state suffixes, handle accents, common aliases (\"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" → \"Corinthians\"?). The BR-Football-Dataset uses unaccented names (\"Sao Paulo\", \"Gremio\"?). Need accent-insensitive matching — normalize by removing accents and lowercasing for matching keys.\n   - queries: match queries, team stats, head-to-head, player queries, standings, biggest wins, averages.\n\n3. Tools to expose via MCP:\n   - search_matches (team, opponent, competition, season, date range, round/stage)\n   - head_to_head (team_a, team_b)\n   - team_stats (team, season, competition, venue)\n   - player_search (name, nationality, club, position, top_n)\n   - standings (competition, season)\n   - biggest_wins (competition, season, n)\n   - average_goals / league_stats (competition, season)\n   - team_info? maybe last match between teams covered by search_matches sorted desc.\n\n4. Tests with pytest + BDD-style scenarios (given/when/then as test names/docstrings). The spec says \"Use BDD test scenarios\" with Gherkin. I'll write tests with Gherkin-style docstrings mapping to pytest functions. Maybe also support `behave`? Not installed. Just pytest with scenario docstrings.\n\nLet me check a few more things:\n- prompts.txt content (maybe sample questions to answer)\n- brazilian-soccer-mcp-guide.md (spec)\n- Data quirks: check distinct rounds in Copa do Brasil (\"Final\"?), stages in Libertadores, tournaments in BR-Football-Dataset, team name forms in each file, FIFA Club values (\"Flamengo\"?), encoding of novo file (it shows Grêmio properly — check if it's utf-8).\n\nAlso check tail of each file to ensure no weird summary rows. Let me also check row counts.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b67fc0d001RW2r56tzUgXIlZ","sessionID":"ses_fa4988af5ffe6mID1MhYqXoot3","messageID":"msg_05b67c9ee001xTwj21hsji4ff8"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788239937424,"sessionID":"ses_fa4988af5ffe6mID1MhYqXoot3","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a9e7e34a1f33167f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"head -60 brazilian-soccer-mcp-guide.md; echo; echo ...; grep -n \"^##\" brazilian-soccer-mcp-guide.md | head -30"},"output":"# Brazilian Soccer MCP Server - Specification\n\n## Overview\n\nThis document specifies the requirements for creating an MCP (Model Context Protocol) server that provides a knowledge graph interface for Brazilian soccer data. The server should enable natural language queries about players, teams, matches, and competitions using the provided datasets.\n\n---\n\n## Purpose\n\n- **Use Case**: Demo/Non-commercial use\n- **Goal**: Create an MCP server that can answer natural language questions about Brazilian soccer\n- **Integration**: MCP server connected to an LLM for query processing\n\n---\n\n## Provided Data\n\nThe following datasets are included in `data/kaggle/` and must be used:\n\n### Match Data\n\n#### 1. Brasileirão Serie A Matches\n**File**: `Brasileirao_Matches.csv` (4,180 matches)\n**Source**: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/ricardomattos05/jogos-do-campeonato-brasileiro\n**License**: CC BY 4.0\n\n| Column | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| datetime | Match date and time |\n| home_team | Home team name with state suffix (e.g., \"Palmeiras-SP\") |\n| home_team_state | State abbreviation |\n| away_team | Away team name with state suffix |\n| away_team_state | State abbreviation |\n| home_goal | Goals scored by home team |\n| away_goal | Goals scored by away team |\n| season | Year of the season |\n| round | Match round number |\n\n#### 2. Copa do Brasil Matches\n**File**: `Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv` (1,337 matches)\n**Source**: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/ricardomattos05/jogos-do-campeonato-brasileiro\n**License**: CC BY 4.0\n\n| Column | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| round | Cup round |\n| datetime | Match date and time |\n| home_team | Home team name |\n| away_team | Away team name |\n| home_goal | Home team goals |\n| away_goal | Away team goals |\n| season | Year |\n\n#### 3. Copa Libertadores Matches\n**File**: `Libertadores_Matches.csv` (1,255 matches)\n**Source**: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/ricardomattos05/jogos-do-campeonato-brasileiro\n**License**: CC BY 4.0\n\n| Column | Description |\n\n...\n3:## Overview\n9:## Purpose\n17:## Provided Data\n21:### Match Data\n23:#### 1. Brasileirão Serie A Matches\n40:#### 2. Copa do Brasil Matches\n55:#### 3. Copa Libertadores Matches\n70:#### 4. Extended Match Statistics\n89:#### 5. Historical Brasileirão (2003-2019)\n108:### Player Data\n110:#### 6. FIFA Player Database\n132:## Additional Data Sources (Optional)\n136:### API-Football\n141:### TheSportsDB\n146:### Wikipedia/DBpedia\n151:## Required Capabilities\n155:### 1. Match Queries\n178:### 2. Team Queries\n200:### 3. Player Queries\n227:### 4. Competition Queries\n248:### 5. Statistical Analysis\n275:## Sample Questions and Expected Behaviors\n277:### Simple Lookups\n284:### Relationship Queries\n291:### Analytical Queries\n300:## Data Quality Notes\n302:### Team Name Variations\n310:### Date Formats\n317:### Character Encoding\n326:## Success Criteria\n","metadata":{"output":"# Brazilian Soccer MCP Server - Specification\n\n## Overview\n\nThis document specifies the requirements for creating an MCP (Model Context Protocol) server that provides a knowledge graph interface for Brazilian soccer data. The server should enable natural language queries about players, teams, matches, and competitions using the provided datasets.\n\n---\n\n## Purpose\n\n- **Use Case**: Demo/Non-commercial use\n- **Goal**: Create an MCP server that can answer natural language questions about Brazilian soccer\n- **Integration**: MCP server connected to an LLM for query processing\n\n---\n\n## Provided Data\n\nThe following datasets are included in `data/kaggle/` and must be u