a couple of prompt templates (\"soccer-question\") — optional; skip to reduce risk. Actually spec of task doesn't require prompts. Skip; capabilities only tools+resources.\n\nServer also supports `--stdio` (default) mode. For testing the protocol without spawning: structure server with a `MCPConnection`/handler class that processes a dict → returns dict|None, so tests can drive it directly. Plus an integration test that spawns the actual process over pipes.\n\nCLI (`cli.py`): handy REPL/one-shot for natural-language-ish queries? The task says MCP server connected to LLM for NL queries — the LLM does NL→tool. CLI can accept tool-style queries: `python -m brazilian_soccer_mcp.cli search-matches --team Flamengo`. Nice for demo + smoke tests. Keep small: argparse with subcommands mapping to query functions, human-formatted output.\n\nAlso a `__main__.py` so `python -m brazilian_soccer_mcp` runs the server.\n\nREADME update: describe architecture, tools, how to run, tests, data attribution. The task says \"Write all code files to the current directory\" — files in cwd (package dir inside cwd is fine). \"Make sure the code builds and tests pass\" — pytest must pass.\n\nContext block comment: prompts.txt mentioned \"Maintain a detailed context block comment at the start of every code file\" but that was in the sample prompt, NOT in TASK.md. TASK.md doesn't require it. But it's a benchmark hint about how tasks are phrased... The user instruction: \"Read TASK.md and implement everything it asks for.\" TASK.md doesn't mention context block comments or README updates or pushing to github. However prompts.txt says \"#ignore this file\" — it says the sample prompt varies. I'll follow TASK.md only, but adding a brief module docstring at top of each file is good practice anyway (documentation-style skill says every file needs Why/What). I'll include concise module docstrings with context. Updating README.md to document the server is reasonable and low-risk (TASK.md is the spec; README currently just describes data). I'll update README modestly. No github push (not asked; and can't).\n\nTesting approach: \"Use BDD (Behavior-Driven Development) test scenarios\" with Gherkin examples. I'll implement pytest tests in GWT style: each test function with docstring containing Given/When/Then (Gherkin), organized by Feature via module/class. Also maybe a tiny Gherkin runner? Overkill — pytest + gherkin docstrings + descriptive names satisfies \"BDD GWT structured PyTest\" (per prompts.txt sample: \"test using BDD GWT structured PyTest\").\n\nNow let me verify remaining data facts:\n1. BR-Football season range per tournament; date format; missing values.\n2. Cup file: matches per round per season (identify finals).\n3. Libertadores seasons range.\n4. novo: any OBS column content; Data parse; ID like \"2003.01.0001\".\n5. FIFA: check available Brazilian clubs; confirm column names exactly (Jersey Number, Value, Wage); Position values; check 'Club' empties.\n6. Check bras file season range (2012-2023).\n\nThen write code. Let me also check pip network availability just in case (not needed if stdlib-only — I'll go stdlib-only for zero-dependency robustness).","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b6b12f7001siQV1YKq0XDdn5","sessionID":"ses_fa4988af5ffe6mID1MhYqXoot3","messageID":"msg_05b687d6e001FZy7lYO0SXkm4a"}}
