one.\n- ('novo hamburggo'...) fine.\n- ('tombeense'...) fine.\n- ('serrinha'?) no.\n- ('souza','PB') fine (\"Souza - PB\", brf 'Sousa EC' → 'sousa' — spelling variation souza vs sousa! Both PB, same club (Sousa-PB). Alias ('sousa','PB')→('souza','PB')? or unify to 'sousa'. Add alias ('sousa','PB')→('souza','PB').)\n- ('humaita'...) fine.\n- ('tuna luso',None) fine.\n- ('falcon'...) fine.\n- ('azuriz'...) fine.\n- ('amazonas',None) fine (AM implied; stateless in brf only).\n- ('monsoon'...) fine.\n- ('macae'...) fine.\n\nCountry codes in lib: 'EQU' (Ecuador), 'URU', 'PAR', 'PER', 'VEN'. Also 'Bol'...? list shows Barcelona-EQU, Nacional-URU, Nacional-PAR, Guaraní-PAR, Libertad-PAR, Olimpia-PAR, Delfín-EQU, Universitario-PER, Trujillanos (VEN), River Plate-URU, Nacional-URU. Also 'Guaraní (PAR)'. OK COUNTRIES = {'uru','par','equ','per','ven'}.\n\nAlso lib \"Athletico\" alias done. lib \"Botafogo\" stateless → prominence RJ ✓. lib \"Corinthians\", \"Palmeiras\", \"São Paulo\" stateless → SP ✓. lib \"Fortaleza\" stateless → CE ✓. lib \"Red Bull Bragantino\" → alias → (bragantino, SP) ✓.\n\nAlso \"Independiente Del Valle\" vs \"Independiente del Valle\" — case fine after lowercase. \"Newells Old Boys\" fine. \"Jorge Wilstermann\" fine. \"LDU\" fine (\"LDU\" = Liga Deportiva Universitaria de Quito; brf? no). \"Junior de Barranquilla\" vs \"Junior\"? only one. \"Colón\" fine. \"Huachipato\" fine.\n\nDedup key for matches: (competition, season, date, home_key, away_key). Where club key = core|state. \n\nHmm wait, one more dedup subtlety: novo 2012-2019 vs bras 2012-2021 — same matches. Date equality: novo Data \"20/05/2012\" → 2012-05-20; bras datetime \"2012-05-20 ...\" → date part. Equal ✓. But do the two datasets always agree on the exact date? If a match was postponed... both reflect final. Probably agree. If they disagree, we'd get duplicates — tests will check e.g. count of Serie A 2019 matches = 380.\n\nLet me quickly verify overlap dedup numbers with a prototype before writing the full package? I'll just build the real thing and assert in tests; if 2019 != 380 I'll investigate. Actually let me pre-verify now with a quick script implementing minimal parsing for the three serie-a sources to see if dates align. Worth 2 minutes to avoid redesign. Also check states: novo gives UF for teams; for dedup key I need (core|state): novo Grêmio+RS; bras Gremio-RS+RS ✓ same.\n\nPotential mismatch: novo team \"Athletico-PR\" vs bras \"Atletico-PR\" → alias both to (atletico, PR) ✓. novo \"América-MG\" UF MG vs bras \"America-MG\" → deaccent ✓.\n\n### Competition normalization\n\n```\nCOMP_ALIASES = {\n 'serie a': series of aliases: 'brasileirao','brasileirao serie a','campeonato brasileiro','serie-a','a','brasileirão','serie a (brasileirao)'...\n}\n```\nMap normalized text → canonical id: 'serie_a','serie_b','serie_c','copa_do_brasil','libertadores'. Also 'all'/'*' → all. Display names + descriptions.\n\n### models.py\n\n```python\n@dataclass Match:\n    competition: str  # canonical id\n    competition_display: str\n    season: Optional[int]\n    date: Optional[date]\n    datetime: Optional[datetime]?  # keep time string\n    time: Optional[str]\n    home: str   # display name\n    away: str\n    home_key: str\n    away_key: str\n    home_goals: Optional[int]\n    away_goals: Optional[int]\n    round: Optional[str]   # round number or stage name\n    venue: Optional[str]   # novo Arena\n    source: str  # file basename\n    stats: Optional[dict]  # corners/shots/attacks/ht\n    def to_dict(...)\n@dataclass Player: id, name, age, nationality, overall, potential, club, position, jersey, foot, value, wage, height, weight, skills: dict\n@dataclass Club: key, core, state, country, display, variants, match_count, competitions, seasons\n@dataclass StandingRow: pos, team, played, w, d, l, gf, ga, gd, pts, note\n```\n\n### loader.py\n\n- `DATA_DIR` resolution: default `<package_dir>/../data/kaggle` — wait, package is in cwd, data in cwd/data/kaggle. Package at ./brazilian_soccer_mcp; data dir = Path(__file__).parent.parent / 'data' / 'kaggle'. But if installed elsewhere... provide `load_all(data_dir=None)`. Also allow env var BRAZILIAN_SOCCER_DATA_DIR. Tests run from repo root — fine.\n- Load functions per file, each robust to missing file (raise clear error).\n- Date parsing: `parse_date(s)`: try formats: '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S', '%Y-%m-%d', '%d/%m/%Y'. Return date. Season fallback: year from date.\n- Int parsing for goals: strip; handle '', '-', 'NA' → None; float-ish '1.0' → int.\n- Build matches, then club registry, then resolve club display names (revisit matches to set display names from registry), build competition match lists with dedup.\n\nOrder: \n1. Parse all rows into Match objects with raw team strings + parsed keys (need parser only, not registry).\n2. Build registry from raw names (count per key, track core→keys).\n3. Resolve display names: for each club key pick display. Then update matches' home/away display to registry display.\n4. Dedup per competition with source priority: priority order: bras(0), novo(1), cup(0), lib(0), brf(2). Wait novo after bras but before brf. For copa: cup(0) then brf(2). OK global priority dict by source: {'Brasileirao_Matches.csv':0, 'novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv':1, 'Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv':0, 'Libertadores_Matches.csv':0, 'BR-Football-Dataset.csv':2}. Sort all matches by (priority, anything) then add to dedup dict keyed (comp, season, date, home_key, away_key). Note: same fixture in bras (round 22) and novo (Rodada 22) — dedup keeps bras. Good.\n   Hmm — wait: is it possible bras and novo have same fixture but different dates (e.g. timezone or data error)? If so dupes remain. Check with prototype.\n5. Also keep `all_matches` (deduped union across competitions — dedup keys already include comp so union = concat of comp lists; but same match can't be in two comps... a Brasileirão match is only serie_a. OK).\n6. Season indexes: per competition dict season → matches; plus per club index: club_key → list of matches (for fast team queries). Build dict club_key → matches. 24k matches × 2 lookups fine.\n\nPlayers: parse fifa_data.csv with utf-8-sig. Filter columns. Value/Wage parse: '€110.5M' → 110_500_000; '€565K' → 565_000. Provide parsed numeric `value_eur`/`wage_eur` optional. Keep raw too.\n\nDataset object exposes query methods? I'll put queries in queries.py taking dataset.\n\n### queries.py\n\nAll return dict (JSON-ready) with a `summary` string + structured data. The MCP tool layer renders text from these. Formatting helpers in `formatting.py`? Put render functions in tools.py or queries returns both structured + formatted text. I'll have queries return structured dicts and a separate `render.py` for human-formatted text (shared by CLI + MCP). Cleaner: tools.py does rendering via render.py functions per tool. Let me consolidate: `render.py` with functions render_matches, render_h2h, etc. Both MCP tools and CLI use them.\n\nQuery functions:\n\n```python\ndef search_matches(ds, team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, stage=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, limit=20) -> dict\n  # team matches (either side); opponent filters to matches vs that team; competition canonical; stage: round number (copa/bras) or stage name (lib: group stage/round of 16/quarterfinals/semifinals/final); special stage 'final' → libertadores stage=='final'; copa → max round per season; serie_a round number.\n  # returns matches sorted desc by date, total_count, truncated flag\ndef last_match_between(ds, team_a, team_b) -> dict | None\ndef head_to_head(ds, team_a, team_b, competition=None) -> dict  # wins/draws/losses for a, goals, list of matches\ndef team_stats(ds, team, season=None, competition=None, venue='all') -> dict  # W/D/L GF GA, per venue home/away, win rate, matches list count\ndef team_profile(ds, team) -> dict  # competitions played with counts and season ranges, total record, aliases seen\ndef player_search(ds, name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_age=None, order='overall', limit=20) -> dict\ndef player_club_summary(ds, club=None, nationality=None)?  # \"Brazilian players at Brazilian clubs: Flamengo: 8 players...\" → tool brazilian_players_by_club? I'll implement as part of player_search result when nationality=Brazil & club grouping... Add separate function `players_grouped_by_club(ds, nationality=...)` returning per-club counts+avg. Provide as its own tool `player_club_report`. Good for spec example.\ndef standings(ds, competition, season) -> dict  # rows, champion, relegated (bottom4 for serie a/b; note), note about source\ndef biggest_wins(ds, competition=None, season=None, limit=10) -> dict\ndef competition_stats(ds, competition=None, season=None) -> dict  # matches, avg goals, home win %, draw %, away win %, per-competition breakdown when no comp given\ndef derbies(ds, season=None, limit=...) -> dict  # curated derby pairs (by club key pairs), matches between them\ndef list_teams(ds, competition=None, season=None) -> dict  # team directory\ndef list_competitions(ds) -> dict  # competitions with season ranges & match counts\n```\n\nDerbies list (curated, key-pairs):\n- Fla-Flu: flamengo| RJ vs fluminense|RJ\n- Clássico dos Milhões: flamengo vs vasco\n- Derby Paulista: palmeiras vs corinthians\n- Majestoso: sao paulo vs corinthians\n- Choque-Rei: palmeiras vs sao paulo\n- Clássico Vovô: fluminense vs vasco\n- Gre-Nal: internacional vs gremio\n- Clássico Mineiro: atletico-mg vs cruzeiro (\"atletico|MG\" vs cruzeiro|RS? cruzeiro state MG! cruzeiro|MG)\n- Clássico Atletiliana? no.\n- Ba-Vi: bahia|BA vs vitoria|BA\n- Clássico dos Gigantes? no. \n- Clássico Re-Pa: remo|PA vs paysandu|PA\n- Ceará derby (Clássico-Rei): ceara|CE vs fortaleza|CE\n- Clássico-Alvinegro? no.\n- Clássico da Amizade? skip.\n- Atletiba: atletico|PR vs coritiba|PR\n- Clássico Véio? skip.\n- Singani? no.\nThat's a solid list of 12. \n\nStandings details: compute table from matches with final scores; teams = all that appear; sort pts desc, wins desc, gd desc, gf desc, name. Champion = first. Relegation: for serie_a mark bottom 4 (criteria: bottom 4 since 2008ish; 2003-2005 varied (2 or 4?) — 2003: relegated 2? Hmm. I'll mark bottom 4 with note \"typical\"). I'll note relegated = bottom 4 (modern standard). For copa/libertadores no standings — return error/note (knockout). For serie_a use deduped comp matches. Validation: 2019 Flamengo champion 90 pts (spec example says Flamengo 90). Test: 2019 standings flamengo pts == 90, champion. Also 2018: Palmeiras 80 pts champion. Good test cases.\n\nWait — the spec example shows 2019: \"1. Flamengo - 90 pts (28W, 6D, 4L)\". 28*3+6=90 ✓. Our data should reproduce.\n\nteam_stats venue: 'home','away','all'. Corinthians home 2022 → from bras 2022 (source bras covers 2022 ✓ 19 home matches).\n\nplayer_search: name substring match (case & accent insensitive), nationality exact-ish (normalize), club resolve via registry then match FIFA club rows by normalized club name in club's candidate names OR core match. Since FIFA club \"Grêmio\" → (gremio, None) core 'gremio' → matches club (gremio, RS) core. Implement: resolve_fifa_club(club_name) → core; match players whose club core == core (state ignored) — collisions like 'botafogo' RJ/SP/PB: FIFA \"Botafogo\" → prominence RJ. Fine.\n\nPosition filter: exact code or group ('GK','DEF'=['CB','LB','RB','LWB','RWB','LCB','RCB'],'MID'=['CDM','CM','CAM','RM','LM','LDM','RDM','LCM','RCM','LAM','RAM'],'FWD'=['ST','LW','RW','CF','LS','RS','LF','RF']). Support group names. \"Show me all forwards from São Paulo FC\" — São Paulo not in FIFA clubs... will return empty with note. Fine — answer honestly from data (the spec example expects grouping across clubs).\n\nOrder options: overall, potential, age, name.\n\nMCP tools list (final):\n1. search_matches\n2. last_match_between\n3. head_to_head\n4. team_stats\n5. team_profile\n6. standings\n7. biggest_wins\n8. competition_stats\n9. derbies\n10. player_search\n11. player_club_report\n12. list_competitions\n13. list_teams\n\nThat covers all required capability categories.\n\nResources:\n- `brazilian-soccer://overview` — datasets summary + schema + team name normalization notes.\n- `brazilian-soccer://competitions` — competitions, seasons, match counts.\n- `brazilian-soccer://teams`? could be big (300+ teams) — fine as text list with match counts.\n- `brazilian-soccer://dataset/{name}` for each of 6 files: columns, row count, license, source URL.\n\n### server.py\n\n```python\nclass BrazilianSoccerMCPServer:\n    PROTOCOL_VERSION = \"2024-11-05\"\n    def __init__(self, dataset=None): lazy load dataset on first tools/resources call (fast startup for initialize handshake)\n    def handle_message(self, msg: dict) -> Optional[dict]\n    # methods: initialize, ping, tools/list, tools/call, resources/list, resources/read, notifications/*\ndef serve(stdin, stdout)  # read lines, dispatch, write responses\ndef main(argv=None)  # argparse: --stdio default; maybe --transport stdio only\n```\n\nJSON-RPC details: id may be int/str/null; respond {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":id,\"result\":...} or error {\"code\":...,\"message\":...}. Parse errors → respond id null error -32700. Batch? MCP doesn't use batch (spec says client MUST NOT send batch... actually older JSON-RPC allows; MCP 2024-11-05 doesn't support batching). If input not array — handle single objects only; if array, process each? Spec: \"requests MAY be batched\"? In MCP 2025-03-26 batching removed; 2024-11-05: JSON-RPC batching is... The 2024-11-05 spec doesn't explicitly forbid; I'll support arrays gracefully (respond with array of non-None responses). Low cost.\n\nAlso handle \"notifications/cancelled\" (ignore), \"notifications/initialized\" (ignore), \"logging\"? no.\n\nError codes: -32700 parse, -32600 invalid request, -32601 method not found, -32602 invalid params, -32603 internal, tool errors → result.isError=true.\n\ninitialize result:\n```json\n{\"protocolVersion\": echo client's if supported else \"2024-11-05\",\n \"capabilities\": {\"tools\": {\"listChanged\": false}, \"resources\": {\"subscribe\": false, \"listChanged\": false}},\n \"serverInfo\": {\"name\": \"brazilian-soccer-mcp\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\"},\n \"instructions\": \"...\" }\n```\nSupported versions: {\"2024-11-05\",\"2025-03-26\",\"2025-06-18\"} — echo whichever client sends (all use same message shapes we implement). Safe.\n\nIf client sends a request before initialize — MCP says server SHOULD error with -32002. Implement lightly: allow anyway? I'll implement the -32002 for non-initialize requests before initialize per spec (Server SHOULD respond with error). Keep it simple but correct.\n\ntools/call result content: text blocks. I'll also include \"structuredContent\"? Only 2025-06-18 supports structuredContent — to stay version-safe, put JSON in text (text = human-readable summary + JSON payload? For LLM consumption human-readable is best; but programmatic tests want structure. Compromise: text = rendered human summary; plus a second text block with JSON? Ugly. Better: text = human summary; and when protocolVersion >= 2025-06-18 add structuredContent. Hmm — simpler: always include `structuredContent` field too (unknown fields ignored by older clients; the field is only *required* to be understood by 2025-06-18 clients — including it always is harmless since it's just an extra result field). I'll include structuredContent always + text rendering.\n\nLine handling: stdin may deliver messages with \\r\\n — strip. Skip empty lines. Write response + '\\n', flush. Also log to stderr optionally (never stdout!). \n\nAlso support `--help`, and graceful shutdown on EOF. Notifications (no id) → no response. For requests during shutdown? N/A.\n\nTesting server: unit-drive `handle_message` dict-in/dict-out; integration: spawn `python -m brazilian_soccer_mcp --stdio` via subprocess, write initialize + initialized + tools/list + tools/call lines, read responses. \n\n### cli.py\n\nargparse subcommands mapping to queries with rendered output:\n- search-matches --team --opponent --competition --season --stage --from --to --limit\n- h2h TEAM_A TEAM_B\n- team-stats TEAM [--season] [--competition] [--venue]\n- team-info TEAM\n- standings COMPETITION SEASON\n- players [--name] [--nationality] [--club] [--position] [--min-overall] [--limit]\n- biggest-wins [--competition] [--season] [--limit]\n- stats [--competition] [--season]\n- derbies [--season]\n- competitions\n- teams [--competition] [--season]\n- serve (default when no subcommand? no — default to server via __main__; cli explicit)\n\n### Performance\n\nLoading: parse ~24k CSV rows + 18k players — well under 1s. Queries linear scans — ms. Standings over 380 matches fine. Biggest wins over 24k — fine.\n\n### README update\n\nDocument: overview, quickstart (run MCP server via stdio, e.g. claude mcp add config JSON example), tools table, data sources & licenses, normalization notes, testing (pytest), performance, project layout. Update the existing README (keep data sources section).\n\n### Context block comments\n\nprompts.txt's sample prompt mentions \"Maintain a detailed context block comment at the start of every code file\" — the user's instruction says implement TASK.md; TASK.md doesn't ask for that. But there's a documentation-style skill in my environment... The available skill \"documentation-style\" says use when writing any documentation artifact. Module docstrings: I'll write a compact header docstring per file with Purpose/Data/Notes — satisfies both good practice and hints. Not overloading.\n\nLet me also double check pytest works and think about the GWT structure. I'll write tests with `Feature:` module docstrings and scenario docstrings:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nFeature: Match Queries\n  As a soccer fan I want to query matches by team, date, competition and season\n  so that I can explore the Brazilian soccer knowledge graph.\n\"\"\"\n