e: core_states['juventude'] = {'RS': count} → RS ✓.\n\nWait — is core_states possibly EMPTY or wrong because of the novo_uf fix function? No...\n\nYou know what? Let me just test resolve() directly in a tiny snippet to see what it produces for the 2021 brf names. Quick empirical check with the exact same code copied. Actually — better idea: I'll write the real normalizer.py + loader.py NOW (they're fully designed), and then debug with the real code + a focused test. The scratch is error-prone; the real code will have clean unit tests for exactly these cases. If 2021 still ≠ 380, I'll debug the real code.\n\nOne thing I want to double-check empirically first (cheap): maybe the dominance resolution DID work and the extra orientations come from something else entirely — e.g. TWO different clubs sharing a core where dominance picked ONE for both: 2021 Serie A has 'America-MG'... hmm no.\n\nActually — one more candidate: 'atletico|PR' vs 'atletico|GO' vs 'atletico|MG' from brf 'Atletico Paranaense'/'Atletico Goianiense'/'Atletico Mineiro' — resolved via ALIAS to PR/GO/MG ✓... but ALSO brf has bare 'Atletico'?? No (2021 debug shows no bare atletico).\n\nHmm — and 2009: 379! One SHORT. That's novo-only season... 2009 Serie A had 380. One match missing in novo data? Or my league rule dropped one: two rows same orientation different score → keep best → still one row... 379 groups = one orientation MISSING in novo 2009 — i.e., novo 2009 itself lacks a match (data gap: 2009 had a match... perhaps the Sport x ... canceled?). Actually — real fact: 2009 Série A had 380 matches; but there was one match... hmm, I recall the 2009 round with São Paulo... Let me count novo 2009 rows directly. Similarly 2016: 381 — one extra vs 380: 2016 had the Chapecoense cancellation — the unplayed Chapecoense vs Atlético-MG... but 381 = 380+1: probably the (0-0 awarded?) vs bras NA row both kept?? League rule keeps ONE per orientation — 381 orientations means an orientation exists that shouldn't... e.g. novo recorded Chapecoense vs Atletico-MG 0-0 AND Atletico-MG vs Chapecoense reversed? Or a data error.\n\nOK — decision: write the real code now. The real loader will:\n- League dedup: one row per (comp, season, hk, ak) — primary = first source-with-goals by priority; fill round/venue from siblings.\n- Expose per-season counts in tests; assert 2003-2013 == known values; for 2014+ assert == 380 EXCEPT where data genuinely lacks (2009: 379, 2016: 381, 2023: 377) — I'll first verify these anomalies are data-truth (count distinct orientations per source) and then decide assertions. For 2019 (407) and 2021 (457) I MUST debug until they're 380 — those look like resolution bugs.\n\nLet me quickly check with the debug-style script: distinct orientations in 2021 from each source individually:\n- bras 2021: 38 teams? 2021 Serie A had 20 teams → 380 orientations from bras alone.\n- brf 2021: also 380.\nIf resolve() merged correctly, union = 380. The scratch gave 457 → union has 457 distinct resolved pairs → resolution failing for ~19 clubs×2 directions×2... 77 extra ≈ (457-380). If 19 clubs failed resolution, extra orientations ≈ 38 (their home+away orientations appear as separate keys) + matches between two phantom clubs also distinct... Actually if brf's 'flamengo|' (stateless, unresolved) coexists with bras 'flamengo|RJ', extra orientations = all orientations involving a phantom key from brf side ≈ 38 per phantom club. 77 extra ≈ 2 phantom clubs! So only ~2 clubs failed resolution in the full scratch. Which two? Ones where ALIAS/DOMINANCE failed: maybe 'atletico goianiense' — my ALIAS key ('atletico goianiense', None) — in the LAST scratch I see `('atletico goianiense',None):('atletico','GO'),('atletico goianiense','GO'):('atletico','GO')` ✓ present. Hmm 'sport recife' ✓ present. 'athletico paranaense' — the dict literal in my last run — I literally cannot remember if ('athletico paranaense',None) survived. I see in the submitted code: `('athletico paranaense','PR'):('atletico','PR'),('athletico paranaense',None):('atletico','PR'),('athletico paranaense',None):('atletico','PR'),` — no...\n\nWhatever — 2019: 407 = 27 extra ≈ 1 phantom club (38 - some overlap). 2019 brf names: 'Athletico Paranaense' stateless... if ('athletico paranaense', None) alias was MISSING in the last run (my typo'ed literal had only the 'PR' variant + duplicates), then brf 2019-2021 'Athletico Paranaense' → pre 'athletico paranaense' st None → no alias → form strip (no form words) → core 'athletico paranaense' → ALIAS (core,st)=('athletico paranaense',None) — the SECOND lookup! If that's missing too → CORE_ALIAS no ('athletico paranaense' not in CORE_ALIAS) → stateless ('athletico paranaense', None) → dominance: core_states['athletico paranaense'] — does any suffixed variant produce core 'athletico paranaense'?? NO — all suffixed ones go through ALIAS to 'atletico'. So dominance has nothing → stays stateless → PHANTOM 'athletico paranaense|'! And cup 'Athletico Paranaense - PR' → pre+PR → ALIAS ('athletico paranaense','PR') → I DID add that at the end: `ALIAS[('athletico paranaense','PR')]=('atletico','PR')` ✓. So cup resolves, brf stateless does NOT (missing (…,None) alias) → phantom → 2019: 27 extra (brf 2019 has 19 Athletico matches + cup... hmm whatever) ≈ consistent!\n\nAnd 2021 457-380=77: phantom 'athletico paranaense|' (brf 38 matches) + maybe 'sport recife' alias present ✓... 77 ≈ 38+38+1? Two phantoms: 'athletico paranaense' AND ... 'atletico mineiro'? ('atletico mineiro',None) IS in the literal ✓. Hmm 'vasco da gama'? brf 'Vasco Da Gama RJ' suffixed ✓. \n\nFINE. The real implementation will have a complete, unit-tested alias table — including ('athletico paranaense', None) → ('atletico','PR'). Enough scratching; the design is validated. 2019/2021 will land at 380 with complete aliases. 2009: 379 and 2016: 381 — I'll verify against raw data (data truth) when writing tests: count distinct orientations in novo 2009 directly and bras+novo 2016.\n\nLet me quickly verify those two anomalies from raw source data (30 seconds), then write the package for real.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b7b4349001705krPnpLbT9UD","sessionID":"ses_fa4988af5ffe6mID1MhYqXoot3","messageID":"msg_05b7a21820016QK2GERaPwrWPr"}}
